1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Amalia Lindegren, (22 May 1814 – 27 December 1891) was a Swedish artist and painter. She was a member of the Royal Swedish Academy of Arts (1856).
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Lindegren was born in Stockholm. At the age of three, she was left an orphan after her mother's death and adopted by the widow of her alleged biological father, Benjamin Sandel. Her position as a child was somewhat humiliating, as
2 CONC  a form of charity object for the upper classes, and in her later work, her paintings of sad little girls are believed to be inspired by her childhood.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her drawings made the artist and art teacher Carl Gustaf Qvarnström (1810–1867) include her as one of the four women accepted as students at the Royal Swedish Academy of Arts in 1849, and in 1850, she became the first woman given 
2 CONC an art scholarship from the academy to study art in Paris, where she remained until 1856. In Paris, she became the student of Léon Cogniet and Ange Tissier. She also studied at the Kunstakademie Düsseldorf and in Münich, and visit
2 CONC ed Rome in 1854-55. In 1856, she returned to Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT Amalia Lindegren socialized with famed culture personalities of the era such as Fredrika Bremer, Olof Eneroth, Wendela Hebbe and Sophie Adlersparre, but she was described as a silent and modestly humble introvert, who never marrie
2 CONC d*, had any lovers* or spoke much at social occasions, who: "lived a retiring life without making a fuss about herself, worked hard and was seldom or never satisfied with what she produced".
2 CONT
2 CONT She died in Stockholm.
2 CONT
2 CONT Artist
2 CONT
2 CONT Lindeberg is associated with the Düsseldorf school of painting. She painted portraits and genre and was inspired by Adolph Tidemand, Hans Gude and Per Nordenberg and the contemporary German style. The painting she sent home from h
2 CONC er studies in Paris was a scene of the drinking of alcohol, which according to the academy was "for a woman a surprising motive" and "This drinking scene bears no traces to have been painted by a spinster".
2 CONT
2 CONT As a portrait painter, she was recommended for her talent of observation, and her paintings from Dalarna, and her sentimental paintings of sad little girls (which are thought to be inspired by her childhood) were very popular; Lil
2 CONC lans sista bädd (The last bed of The Little One) was displayed in Paris in 1867, in Philadelphia in 1876 and in Chicago in 1893.
2 CONT
2 CONT Recognitions
2 CONT
2 CONT Amalia Lindegren became an agré in 1853 and in 1856 a member of the Royal Swedish Academy of Arts. She was an honorary member of the British Female artists Society in London, and awarded the Litteris et artibus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Paintings
2 CONT
2 CONT Söndagsafton i en dalastuga, 1860
2 CONT
2 CONT Frukosten, 1866
2 CONT
2 CONT  Lovisa of Sweden, 1873
2 CONT
2 CONT References Carin Österberg (in Swedish) : Svenska kvinnor: föregångare, nyskapare (Swedish women: Predecessors, pioneers) Lund: Signum 1990. (ISBN 91-87896-03-6) http://runeberg.org/sbh/b0071.html
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Wikipedia ...................... Amalia Lindegren socialized with famed culture personalities of the era such as Fredrika Bremer, Olof Eneroth, Wendela Hebbe and Sophie Adlersparre, but she was described as a silent a
2 CONC nd modestly humble introvert, who never married, had any lovers or spoke much at social occasions, who: "lived a retiring life without making a fuss about herself, worked hard and was seldom or never satisfied with what she produc
2 CONC ed".[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT She died in Stockholm. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Added to Geni from Wikipedia by Janet Milburn 10/15/18
