1 NAME Matts /Hansson/
2 GIVN Matts
2 _MARNM Hansson
2 NICK Mats
2 NICK Hanssons
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1620
2 PLAC Gagnef, Dalarna, Sweden
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE BET SEP 1653 AND DEC 1653
2 PLAC New Sweden (now Delaware)
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://www.colonialswedes.org/forefathers/Mattson.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Matts Hansson, who was hired to be a gunner at Fort Christina. They departed from Stockholm on the Charitas among a group of new settlers that included Hansson's brother, Anders Hansson. After five years of service at Fort Christi
2 CONC na, Matts Hansson became a freeman on 1 December 1646. Like other freemen, he soon became unhappy with Governor Printz's harsh treatment of the settlers. On 27 July 1653, he and his brother joined other freemen in filing a complai
2 CONC nt with the Governor. Printz, however, branded the complaint a "mutiny" and left for Sweden, leaving the colony under the rule of his son-in-law Johan Papegoja, threatening to take legal action against the mutineers on his return.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fearing for their lives, many of the freemen who had signed the complaint fled New Sweden in the fall of 1653. Anders Hansson made it safely to Kent Island in the Chesapeake Bay, but Matts Hansson did not. Papegoja hired Indians t
2 CONC o bring back the escapees, dead or alive. Two of the freemen were overtaken and killed, with the Indians bringing back their heads on stakes. One of those killed was Matts Hansson.
2 CONT
2 CONT --
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Sweden, his father's name must have been Hans, since until the early 20th century Scandinavian names changed each generation based on patronyms. Accordingly, in America, his son John received the last name Matson after the
2 CONC  father (meaning the son of Mats). (Source needed verifying that he was in fact the father of John Matson.)
2 CONT
2 CONT --
2 CONT
2 CONT From Martha's Extended Family page on Nils Matson: http://www.martisgenes.info/p315.htm#i8515
