1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [https://www.genealogy.com/forum/surnames/topics/bankston/740/ Lawrence Bankston] b. 1704 and his wife, Rebecca Hendricks.This is the order of birth of five sons; the deposition of James Lea of 1797 clarifies that 
2 CONC the third son was Lawrence Jr., son of Rebecca Hendricks: The third son Lawrence Jr. d. aft 1819 in Spartanburg, SC. 
2 CONT
2 CONT (Note:Lawrence Bankson, Jr has been traced extensively by Cynthia Forde-Beatty and proves there were 5 sons, not 4 and explains how the two Lawrence Bankston have been conflated.
2 CONT Please see sources https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Bankston-488)
2 CONT *http://bankstonblog.blogspot.com/
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrew d. abt 1783 Montgomery County, NC
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter d. abt. 1802 Clarke County, GA
2 CONT
2 CONT Lawrence Jr. d.after August 1765 Court Records -Orange County, North Carolina court records list Andrew BANKSTON, Lawrence BANKSTON Sr. and Lawrence BANKSTON, Jr. (Haigler, op.cit.) and bef. 4 January 1787 ( Wilkes County deed boo
2 CONC k AA -Peter BANKSTON, Jacob BANKSTON and Daniel BANKSTON sell land "willed to our mother Rebecca Hendricks" and is recorded in the State of Pennsylvania, November 27, 1786.Hendricks in Pa. Lawrence's name would have been required 
2 CONC on the land claim and sale if he was living.
2 CONT
2 CONT Daniel
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob (Jaoob is deposed below).
2 CONT
2 CONT The deposition is included in the timeline of Lawrence Bankston below: 
2 CONT TIMELINE LAWRENCE BANKSON -
2 CONT
2 CONT 1704        Birth - Lawrence BANKSON, son of Andrew BANKSON Jr. and Gertrude ( Lars Boore ) , in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1725        Marriage - Rebecca Hendricks b. ca., 1705, daughter of John Hendricks and Rebecca Wells.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1736-37       Jailed - John Hendricks who was jailed in Lancaster, and who was required to provide bail for his and Lawrence BANKSTON's good behavior at Lancaster County Court in early 1737 (they        participated in the riot of
2 CONC  Marylanders, including the sack of Henry Hendricks' (of Tobias, Sr.        house in the Fall of 1736. Lawrence BANKSTON's land claims in York County was by        Maryland warrant; it was worthless after 1769 and had no Pennsylva
2 CONC nia status before. The        1785 Georgia deed of this land claim by BANKSTON's heirs for 10,000 (Continental) dollars        was an exchange of worthlessness for worthlessness. These Hendricks were Quaker        converts , but b
2 CONC ecame Baptists, Dunkers, etc. John, Sr.'s first wife was Frances Bezer;        second wife was Rebecca, widow of Wells.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1740-1744        Migration - (Source: Mona Herrin, a BANKSTON descendant and researcher) "Between 1740-1744, after the death of his wife, Lawrence BANKSTON moved to Edgecombe County, North Carolina. He is listed in the Colonial Ce
2 CONC nsus, November 24, 1744.The early death of Rebecca Hendricks BANKSTON has not beenproved.Anne Martin Haigler says, 'It has been written that Rebecca Hendricks BANKSON (sic) died ca. 1740; however, the deeds in Wilkes County, Georg
2 CONC ia seem to dispute this." (BANKSTON Cousins1656-1996, Anne Martin Haigler, Hardbound, Inc., 737 A. n. Hwy. 67, Florissant, Missouri, 63031, p. 5. )
2 CONT
2 CONT 24 November 1844        Lawrence was admitted tothe Council to prove his rights in Edgecombe County,North Carolina; He received a fifty acre grant for every two members of his family; the grant record could not be located to learn
2 CONC  the total amount of land he received.This land became a part of Granville County, North Carolina in 1746. There are several records showing Lawrence in the area. 1.) Colonial Census, 2.) St. Matthew's Parish Church appointed Lawr
2 CONC ence as a vestryman. (Haigler, op.cit.)
2 CONT
2 CONT 1752-1760        Appointment to Justice of the Peace - Orange County, North Carolina. Information from Michael S. Parks states that Lawrence was one of six original Justices of the Peace when Orange County was first organized in 1
2 CONC 752 and that he served sucessive terms throughout the decades of 1750 and 1760.He owned property in Orange, Edgecombe, and Craven County, as well as the land he inherited in Philadelphia.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1755        Tax List -Orange County, North Carolina - Lawrence BANKSON, Esqr. and sons - 5 white polls. To be counted in the poll, each son had to be over 16 years of age which gives us 4 sons born before 1739.
2 CONT
2 CONT April 1757        Named Road Commissioner Orange County, North Carolina.
2 CONT
2 CONT 23 January 1761        Land Grant: 595 acres in Orange County, North Carolina by Lord Granville. Patent Book 14, p. 343 which was surveyed July 12, 1754.
2 CONT
2 CONT August 1765        Court Records -Orange County, North Carolina court records list Andrew BANKSTON, Lawrence BANKSTON Sr. and Lawrence BANKSTON, Jr. (Haigler, op.cit.)
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 January 1787        Land Sale - Wilkes County deed book AA - Peter BANKSTON, Jacob BANKSTON and Daniel BANKSTON all of Wilkes County sell to Matthew Wood of Greene County, Georgia, 440 acres which came to them from their father,
2 CONC  Lawrence BANKSTON.The same three men sold to Wood 371/2 acres located in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, which was willed 'to our mother, "Rebecca Hendricks and is recorded in the State of Pennsylvania, November 27, 1786.Rebecca Hend
2 CONC ricks could not inherit land in 1786 if she died in 1740 unless it was willed to her or to heirs of her body; otherwise, for Lawrence to have married in 1747 and had four more children, he would have had to divorce Rebecca. Unfort
2 CONC unately, the Archives in Philadelphia could not locate the land deed, nor have I found any record of a divorce. ( Haigler, op.cit. p.5)
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 January 1788       Estate Records - Deed BookJ. {p. 239-240)in Caswell County, North Carolina, James BANKSTON, son of Andrew BANKSON, is proved to be heir-at-law as the grandson of Capt. Lawrence BANKSON, deceased.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1783        Estate Records -Montgomery County North Carolina, Andrew BANKSON is deceased.To be the heir at law, James BANKSTON would have to be the oldest son of the oldest son.
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTES ON THE SONS OF CAPTAIN LAWRENCE BANKSTON -
2 CONT
2 CONT The Ancestry of Peter BANKSTON (1729-1803), p. 5, Dr. Peter S. Craig: "By his marriage to Rebecca Hendricks, Lawrence BANKSTON had four sons, all of whom were probably born in York County, Pennsylvania. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Andrew BANKSTON, born by estimation in 1727, died in Montgomery County, North Carolina, in 1783, when he died intestate.His widow Ann BANKSTON became administratrix of his estate.(The Journal of North Carolina Genealogy, 8:1042
2 CONC .https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Bankston-144
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.Peter BANKSTON, born by estimation in 1729, died intestate in Clarke County, Georgia, in 1803.Administration of his estate was granted to his widow Priscilla. Probate records show that he was survived by four sons (John, Lawrenc
2 CONC e (also called Lary, which some have misread as Levy), William and Andrew) and four daughters, Judith, married to Nimrod Taylor; Rhoda, married to Shadrack Carpenter; another daughter married to Thomas DAVIS, and another married t
2 CONC o William Browning.https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Bankston-173
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Daniel BANKSTON, born by estimation in 1731, died in Morgan County, Georgia, in 1811.His will named his wife Rachel, sons Thomas, Abner, Isaac and Spencer BANKSTON and daughter Edith and Patty.https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Bank
2 CONC ston-314
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Jacob BANKSTON, born by estimation in 1733, died after 1804, probably in Clarke County, Georgia.His sons included John, Jacob, Elijah and Henry.https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Bankston-383
2 CONT
2 CONT (EDIT TO ADD FIFTH SON
2 CONT 5. Lawrence Bankston, Jr. b. 1730 d. Aft 1819 Spartanburg, SC.married Sarah lnu. See extensive sources and research by Cynthia Forde-Beatty. https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Bankston-488)
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Peter S. Craig F.A.S.G., Historian for The Swedish Colonial Society, Washington, D.C., writes, "Despite claims in various BANKSTON genealogies, there were no additional sons.Andrew BANKSTON, the eldest son, had died before 27 
2 CONC November, 1786 when the remaining three BANKSTON brothers sold their parents' lands in two deeds recorded in Wilkes County, Georgia."January 4, 1787, in Wilkes County,deed book AA:127, Peter BANKSTON, Jacob BANKSTON and Daniel BAN
2 CONC KSTON all of Wilkes Co, sell to Matthew Wood of Greene Co, Georgia 440 acres which came to them from their father, Lawrence BANKSTON.The same three men sold to Wood 37 1/2 acres located in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania which was "wil
2 CONC led to our mother, Rebecca Hendricks and is recorded in the State of Pennsylvania, November 27, 1786."This land was three miles above York on the Great Road leading to Comewago, being the same tract run from (surveyed for) our fat
2 CONC her Lawrence BANKSTON by one Thomas Cresap when the (temporary) dividing line was run between Pennsylvania and Maryland. (Wilkes County deeds, BB: 114.
2 CONT
2 CONT These deeds contradict the claims in several BANKSTON genealogies that Lawrence BANKSTON had a second wife (Ann Major) and more sons. Had there been other sons by a second wife, it would have been necessary for them to join in the
2 CONC  second deed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrew BANKSTON, the eldest son, and his brother Peter BANKSTON both served as chain carriers for a land survey in July of 1751 (Hofman, Granville District Land Grants).Daniel BANKSTON and Jacob BANKSTON do not appear until later 
2 CONC records, both being appointed to a road commission in Anson County on 12 October 1771 (Anson County MInutes).
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Craig continues," The 1782 tax list for Montgomery County North Carolina lists Lawrence BANKSTON; it also lists Ann BANKSTON on that same list.She was the widow of Andrew BANKSTON and not the wife of Lawrence BANKSTON.I am 100
2 CONC % certain of the above and find absolutely no evidence that Lawrence BANKSTON married an Ann Major or any other Ann.Indeed, he died well before Vacher's claim. Her "conclusions" seem to be created out of thin air."(February 16, 20
2 CONC 03 letter to Ron S. Beatty)
2 CONT
2 CONT ANNE MARTIN HAIGLER - BANKSTON COUSINS: "The only Ann Majors that I found in SWEEMS (an index to the records of Virginia) are:
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne Major, widow of William Major in 1727, who had several children. Ann Timson, dau. of Ann Thornton and Samuel Timson.she married Samuel Major and married second, Richard Garrett.(Excerpted from "The BANKSTON Cousins" by Anne H
2 CONC aigler, p. 5-6)
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTES ON OTHER SONS: Mrs. Haigler joins Dr. Craig's opinion regarding Edna Vacher's claim of other children said to be of Lawrence BANKSTON Sr. and Ann Major:
2 CONT
2 CONT 32.Lawrence BANKSTON Jr. (Lawrence BANKSTON is the son ofPeter BANKSTON - he did not inherit from Lawrence Sr., but from Peter.) 33.Richard BANKSTON(Vacher's Richard BANKSTON (236) is not proved to be of the same generation.The fi
2 CONC rst appearance of a Richard BANKSTON in Montgomery county North Carolina in the 1800 census suggests that he is of a later generation (Dr. Craig, op.cit.) Mary BANKSTON(Vacher's Mary BANKSTON (237) should be stricken.There are no 
2 CONC records of a Mary BANKSTON at all). 34.John BANKSTONb. MAY 8, 1760d. February. 23, 1838 (this John BANKSTON was Peter's son.)
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTE:Carl Strickland, New Braunfels, Texas, a descendant - writes, "Lawrence BANKSONwas a Captain in the King's Military who moved to Edgecombe County, North Carolina between 1740 and 1744 - followed by a move to New Bern, Craven 
2 CONC County, North Carolina."
2 CONT
2 CONT A huge thank you for a great find from Bess Antes:
2 CONT
2 CONT I made it back to the Library and found the following information, but first, a few side notes about North Carolina.
2 CONT
2 CONT Originally North Carolina was divided into eight sections, Lord Granville of the Granville Grant being one of the eight people who owned bits of the original state. At some point the King decided to buy back the rights and Lord Gr
2 CONC anville refused to do so as he owned the upper half of the state. Well, he (Granville) dallied before getting everything surveyed and in his doing so he created a vacuum in land ownership. For example: Lawrance Bankston applied fo
2 CONC r land in Edgecombe County in 1844 but the Edgecombe Courts could not decide whether or not they had the right to give out Granville's land. Consequently, after a long conversation with Mr. Bridges, I feel that the court probably 
2 CONC denied Lawrance Bankstons 1744 claim for land in Edgecombe County. Then, when he did get a legal grant from Granville, Lawrance moved to what is now present day Caswell County. Also, most people apparently filed for a grant the mo
2 CONC ment they entered the state so Lawrance Bankston probably moved to NC in 1744.
2 CONT
2 CONT Then, after the Revolutionary War, every time land came into dispute (you will read more about that later) people would try to discredit a person by saying that they were Tory Sympathizers and as such had no right to any land they
2 CONC  owned prior to the Revolutionary War. (Tory land was seized after the War.) While I could find no proof today that Andrew Bankston was a Tory keep in mind that his father held the rank of Cap't for the King's government and stood
2 CONC  to loose a lot of his land if Britain lost the War. Also, Robert Lee, Andrew's father-in-law's name (Robert Lee of Anson County) does appear on a list of British Loyalty Claims. These claims were made to the British Government fo
2 CONC r restitution of losses incurred during the war.
2 CONT
2 CONT Happy Reading, Bess in NC
2 CONT
2 CONT 29 day of August 1796 Deposition of Henry Williamson
2 CONT
2 CONT Question put by the defendant:“Was you acquainted with the family of Andrew Bankston any time in your life time? Answer I was.
2 CONT
2 CONT Question: Do you know what name the eldest son of said Andrew went by? Answer Daniel
2 CONT
2 CONT 3rd: Do you know, or did you ever know whether or whether not, is wasever disputed by the eighbors of said Andrew Bankston that his eldest son was not of the name of Dan’l? Answer I did not ever hear of it disputed.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4th Did you never hear a flying hearsay that those person’s were dead? Answer I did hear it, I hear that the said Andrew Bankston and his son Daniel were dead.
2 CONT
2 CONT Question 5th; You say you heard it but do you know of it of your own knowledge? Answer No
2 CONT
2 CONT 6th Was it not a general report among the people who knew the said Andrew Bankston that he went to the British within their lines? Answer: I was away from where I was acquainted with him before I hear’d such news, but after I left
2 CONC  that I’d hear’d such a report.
2 CONT
2 CONT 7th How long since you first hear the report of the death of the said Andrew Bankston? AnswerAlmost thirteen or fourteen years ago, or there about.
2 CONT
2 CONT 8th Can you give any account of how old the said Daniel Bankston, son of Andrew, was when you knew him? Answer I suppose he was about nineteen ortwenty years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT 9thHow near did you live to the family of the said Andrew when you knew him afores’d Daniel Bankston Answer Part of the time about two miles or thereabouts and part of the time about a mile and a half.
2 CONT
2 CONT Questions of behalf of the plaintiff: Question 1st Do you know whether or not the said Andrew Bankston was ever an inhabitant of the waters of Hico Creek? Answer I always understood it so.
2 CONT
2 CONT Question 2nd Did you hear of the said Andrew Bankston say that he held any rights to the piece or parcel of land now is dispute between the plaintiff and the defendant? Answer I don’t recollect that ever I did
2 CONT
2 CONT Question 3 Do you know of the next eldest son of Andrew Bankston to Daniel? Answer James, I always understood to be the next eldest to Daniel
2 CONT
2 CONT Question 4th Have you reason to believe that said Andrew and Dan’l are dead? Answer I have heard it different times and never hear’d it disputed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Question 5th How long since you were first acquainted with the said Dan’l Bankston? Answer I suppose about seventeen or eighteen years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Question 6th Did you ever hear that the property of said Andrew was confiscated after you hear’d he went to the British?Answer No
2 CONT
2 CONT Question Anything further the Deponant sayeth AnswerNot
2 CONT
2 CONT Deposition of Sulliam Williamson 27 August 1796 Question 1st Was you any time in your lifetime acquainted with Andrew Bankston? Answer Yes
2 CONT
2 CONT Question 2 Which of his sons did you understand was the oldest? Answer Daniel
2 CONT
2 CONT Qn 3rd Did you hear that the said Andrewand Daniel his son were dead? Ans Yes, I heard so. Qn 4th Did you know it of your own knowledge? Ans No
2 CONT
2 CONT Qn 5thWas not the general Corector that these two men Torries? Answer -I have heard them supposed to be Torries
2 CONT
2 CONT Qn 6th Did you not apprehend that they were apprehended for suc? AnswerNo
2 CONT
2 CONT Questions asked by the Plaintiff 1st These questions did you ever give them in as heresay or of your own knowledge? Ans: By information of others
2 CONT
2 CONT Qn 2nd Which was the next oldest son to Daniel Answer: I understood James
2 CONT
2 CONT Qn 3 rd Do you know anything of the said land that is now is dispute between the plaintiff and the defendant? Ans: I do not know anything about the right of the (can’t read word).
2 CONT
2 CONT Qn 4th Did you ever hear or know of anything of these said Bankston’s having any right to this said disputed land in their lifetimes? Answer: No
2 CONT
2 CONT Question asked by the defendant, How long do you think is it since you heard of the said Andrew & Daniel’s death? Answer:I suppose it has been thirteen orfourteen years or more. And further this defendant sayeth? Not. ____________
2 CONC ______________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Deposition of James Lee 1 January 1, 1797
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT Question 1st: Was you ever acquainted with the family of Lawrance Bankson? Answer; Yes, for I married a wife out of the family of said Bankson my (Bossom?) friend. Question 2nd:Who by name was the oldest son of him, the said Lawra
2 CONC nce Bankson and heir? Answers: Andrew Bankson was the oldest son and heir of the said Lawrance, deceased. Question 3rd: Was the said Andrew Bankson some time an inhabitant of Hico? Answer: Yes, he lived on Lynches Creek. Question 
2 CONC 4th: Did he not move to the south? Answer: yes, I understand so. Question 5th: Do you know what the name his oldest son was called by? Answer: I do not know whether Daniel or James. Question 6th: Do you know anything of your own k
2 CONC nowledge of the death of said Daniel Bankson, son of Andrew Bankson or James? Answer: I do not. Question 7th: How long do you suppose it to be since the death of the aforesaid Lawrance Bankson? Answer: He died before the war. Ques
2 CONC tion 8th: Do you know any will the said Lawrance Bankson ever made of any of his property? Answer: No, for I do believe that he never made any Question 9th: Was not Jacob Bankston the youngest son of the family of the said Lawranc
2 CONC e Bankson? Answer yes, for Andrew the oldest son (unreadable word), the next Daniel, the next Lawrance Jnr, and Jacob Bankson was the youngest son of awrance Bankson, deceased. Question 10th: Did you believe that you are capable o
2 CONC f attending the Supperior Court? Answer” No, I believe that I am not by the infirmity of old age. Sworn before us the day and year above written, witnessed our hand and seal: Samuel Johnston, Gabriel Lea (Le) James Lee.
2 CONT
2 CONT Deposition of James Gray and Andrew Bankson To James Gray: Do you know the length of your memory that James and Jacob Bankson borne to you with a deed? Answer: yes Ques 2nd: Do you know whether James or Jacob Bankson did take away
2 CONC  a deed from your house without any witnesses? Answer: I do (unreadable word) that James Bankson and Jacob Bankson either brought a deed or come to get one wrote and he also said he believes that James Bankson did assign said deed
2 CONC  but who the deed was too he nows not and the defendant further (cant read word) not.
2 CONT
2 CONT Then the depondant Andrew Bankson sayeth after being sworn in: Question: Do you remember that Jacob Bankson and James was at your house with a deed and wanted and you to goand witness to it?
2 CONT
2 CONT Ans: I do not. I remember hearing my family say that Jacob Bankson and JamesBankson was at my house, and they also said that they were going to JamesGray’s house to get a deed in or June (can’t read word) after they brought a deed
2 CONC  to my house and left it there for some time. And the said Depd. Said that he has reason to believe that it was the same deed that James Gray spoke of and he further sayeth that he remembers that Jacob Bankson told him to get Jame
2 CONC s Bankson to acknowledge said deed and for him to become a witness and he also sayeth that he did give the deed to John W/M??? (can’t read) in the year 1792 and the said S??? inquired the way to James Bankson and I put him on the 
2 CONC way. I understand that then S??? was a going to James Bankson to get him to acknowledge the ??? (can’t read).
2 CONT
2 CONT Questions for the Plaintiff (Bankson) The Depondant saith that he believed that this grant survey by Wm Churlton July 1754 for Capt. Lawrance Bankson lying on both sides of No. Hico …. Is the same grant and that he also sayeththat
2 CONC  he believes that the Cap’t Lawrance Bankson is dead and he has been dead upwards of 20 years and he also sayeth that he rather(??can’t read word) that he never made any will . Also that Andrew Bankson, son of said Lawrance was hi
2 CONC s eldest, and that Daniel Bankson was the oldest son of Andrew Bankson. I believe that the said Andrew Bankson son of Lawrance is dead and I have been credible informed that Daniel is dead also, and also he also sayeth that he thi
2 CONC nks that the said Andrew bankson has been dead 13 or 14 years and that he also sayeth that he heard the said Daniel bankson died shortly after. And he also said that James Bankson was the next eldest son to the said Daniel and he 
2 CONC also sayeth that he believes the said James Bankson is the heir-at-law.
2 CONT
2 CONT Question by the Defendant to the Dep. Bankson: What was you when you heardthat the Said Daniel Bankson was dead? Answer: Montgomery County and I fully understand that the sd in (???can’t read word??)
2 CONT
2 CONT Question by the plaintiff to Bankson: Do you believe that this deed of James Bankson to Jacob dated the first day of January 1788 Answer: I believe that she said James Bankson (can’t read might be aforgnd) the said deed but was n 
2 CONC present and the (???can’t read word??) (???can’t read word??) not.. Signed James Gray and Andrew Bankson
