2 CONT
2 CONT (LindaLabinoriginally submitted this to Labin-Mullenax Family Tree on 18 Jun 2009 Swedish Colonial Historian relates story of Nils Larsson Frande/Friend and lists his offspring and Dr. Labin offers theories on the name Friend.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Nils Larsson Frande/Friend, Progenitor of the Friend Family 1648-1678 , Sweden, NJ, MD, VA, DE, PA
2 CONT
2 CONT Nils Larsson Frande/Friend, Progenitor of the Friend Family--In his article on "Nils Larsson Frande/Friend," Dr. Peter Stebbins Craig, FASG, FGSP, the Historian of the Swedish Colonial Society, provides much valuable information a
2 CONC bout the Friends' progenitor. I have added my own comments about the surname Friend and the theories for the adoption of that name. 
2 CONT
2 CONT "One relatively unknown forefather, progenitor of the Friend family in America, is Nils Larsson, who arrived in New Sweden on the Swan in 1648 and served as a warden for Governor Rising 1654. Two years later, he married Anna Ander
2 CONC sdotter (possibly the daughter of Anders Andersson the Finn) and settled at Upland (now Chester), where they raised a family of ten children. His house was also the location of the Swedes' quarterly court sessions after Armegot Pr
2 CONC intz sold the Printz family's Tinicum Island estate. Nils Larsson played a very prominent role in the Swedish community until his death at Upland in the winter of 1686-87. He became known as Nils Larsson Frände, meaning "kinsman" 
2 CONC or "blood relative" in Swedish, possibly because of his influence among the Indians, who considered him a "blood brother." Under English rule, his adopted surname became anglicized to "Friend." In 1668, Nils and two other Swedes s
2 CONC ecured a permit from the govemor of the new province of New Jersey to buy lands from the Indians in present Gloucester County. The resulting acquisition led to a large Swedish settlement centering around Raccoon Creek (present Swe
2 CONC desboro)."
2 CONT
2 CONT [Notes from Dr. Linda L. Labin: although Nils Larsson Frande may have been friendly with the Indians, other researchers have suggested that he was a "Friend," meaning a Quaker*, and that might be the reason he was given, then assu
2 CONC med, the surname Friend. Unlike other Protestants and Catholics who settled in America, the Quakers tended to view the Indians as humans, equals, and treated them accordingly. As the frontier accepted immigrants from other countri
2 CONC es and religions, people who instead viewed Indians as savages, animals, less than human, in other words, the Indians grew more hostile, encouraged to do so when the French and English in particular began to war over the new colon
2 CONC ies, and rewarded the Indian tribes that waged war on innocent settlers. Some settlers, not so innocent, created more problems by massacreing Indian villages, stealing land and food, and cheating the original inhabitants. The Frie
2 CONC nd family were generally on good terms with the Indians, true "blood brothers," until the French and Indian War instigated an inter-racial war. History shows that many of the early Friend family were respected by the Shawnee, and 
2 CONC were the first--perhaps only--white men given title deeds to the lands they settled by the Indian chieftains. This sets them apart from other settlers, for obvious reasons.]
2 CONT
2 CONT [*While the Quaker theory explaining Nils Larsson's name Friend seems plausible, according to research performed by Dr. Craig, historically speaking, the Quakers did not emigrate to America in large numbers until much later. Dr. C
2 CONC raig,  Don Norman, Warren Skidmore, and others have shown that the majority of Swedish immigrants (including Finns) were members of the Swedish Lutheran Church, not Quakers. While this area still is in need of further research, we
2 CONC  presently must accept the notion that the Friend family worshipped in the Lutheran church (the only one available in New Sweden early on), so probably were not Quakers. Why the "Friend/Frande" appellation, then? I would agree wit
2 CONC h Dr. Craig's argument that "Friend" was added first in the New World, not in Sweden, even though we can't as yet locate Nils Larsson's ancestors in Sweden. My alternative to the Quaker and Indian theories would be that Nils et al
2 CONC . introduced themselves to the Indians in this way--"I'm Nils Larsson, Frande/Friend." (I'm Nils son of Lars, a friend to the Indians). Some researchers think Nils added the name because the Indians considered him a kinsman, a blo
2 CONC od brother, but that is doubtful unless one can prove some sort of inter-marriage with the Indian tribes. They did not award the term "blood brother" willy-nilly; a white man had to earn such a fond term--that took years of honora
2 CONC ble dealings with the Indians, fair trading, and either taking of Indian wives or standing up for the Indians against other tribes/white men. I think Nils attempted to befriend the native Americans and that is the reason he called
2 CONC  himself Friend. Then, his offspring continued to use it and the Indians did so, too, perhaps because he and his children & grandchildren lived up to the name, treating the Indians with kindness and respect.Then, under the influen
2 CONC ce of the newly arrived English and Scots/Scots-Irish settlers, the family adopted the surname Friend to simplify identification.]
2 CONT
2 CONT "Nils Larsson Frände also acquired lands in present Bucks County, which he traded to William Penn in return for 800 acres east of Red Clay Creek in New Castle County. Penn built his Pennsbury estate on Frände's former land. At the
2 CONC  time of his death, Nils Larsson was serving as constable for Chester township. His wife Anna survived him by about 40 years and was said to be over 106 when she died. Their children, with approximate birth years, were:
2 CONT
2 CONT # '''Brigitta''', born 1657, who married John Cock (son of Peter Larsson Cock) and had nine children.
2 CONT # '''Anders''' (Andrew), born 1659, whose first wife was probably a daughter of Israel Helm. [Rebecca Helm? 1670-1703]. '''Andrew''' Friend died in Maryland after 1740, and had at least four children, including Israel Friend, a we
2 CONC ll-known Indian trader and interpreter.
2 CONT # '''Catharine''', born 1661, who married Olof (William) Dalbo, and died at Raccoon Creek in 1721, the mother of nine children.
2 CONT # '''Maria''', born 1663, who married Gabriel Cock (son of Peter Larsson Cock) and had eight children.
2 CONT # '''John''', born 1666, who married Anna (daughter of Hendrick Coleman), and died in Penn's Neck, Salem County, NJ in the winter of 1737-38; nine children.
2 CONT # '''Susannah''', born 1670, who married Enoch Enochson and moved to Gloucester County, NJ; four surviving sons.
2 CONT # '''Sarah''', born 1672, who married Amos Nicholas of Chester County; at least four children.
2 CONT # '''Gabriel''', born 1674, who married Maria Van Culin of Chester County; at least five children.
2 CONT # '''Lawrence''', born 1676, who married Sarah Jaquet in Penn's Neck; at least four children.
2 CONT # '''Barbara''', born 1678, who married Peter Longacre (son of Anders Petersson Longacre); at least three children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Descendants of Nils Larsson Friend have an active Friend Family Association which owns and operates a Friend Family Library in Friendsville, Maryland, where many records of descendants have been collected." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: Swedish Colonial News, Volume 1, Number 3 (Spring 1991), rep. http://www.colonialswedes.o rg/Forefathers/Friend.html 
2 CONT ----
