2 CONT MEMORIAL PHOTOS 1 FLOWERS 4 The first permanent settler in what is present-day Washington County. This stone marks the site on "Swede's Delight". Located at the mouth of the Conococheague Creek in Williamsport. The property was ob
2 CONC tained possibly as early as 1732, when Lord Baltimore offered inducements to homesteaders to settle the western reaches of the Royal Province of Maryland. "Friend's Fort" situated on the site, served jointly as a family residence 
2 CONC and place of refuge for other settlers during the French and Indian War.
2 CONT
